.TH PHASORSTD_STR 3 "August 2026" "Phasor 4.0.0" "Phasor Standard Library 4.0.0"
.SH NAME
phasorstd_str \- Phasor string manipulation functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B include "std/str";
.PP
.B find(string, substr[, start[, end]])
.B len(string)
.B char_at(string, index)
.B substr(string, start[, length])
.B concat(string1, string2, ...)
.B to_upper(string)
.B to_lower(string)
.B starts_with(string, prefix)
.B ends_with(string, suffix)
.B split(string, delimiter)
.PP
.B sb_new()
.B sb_append(handle, string)
.B sb_prealloc(handle, capacity)
.B sb_to_string(handle)
.B sb_clear(handle)
.B sb_free(handle)
.fi
.SH STRING FUNCTIONS
.TP
.BR find (string, substr[, start[, end]])
Find the first occurrence of a substring in a string. Three calling conventions are
supported, and \fBthey do not all behave the same way when the substring is not found\fR
(see NOTES).
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBstring\fR 12
Input string
.IP \fBsubstr\fR 12
Substring to search for
.IP \fBstart\fR 12
Starting index to begin the search from (optional)
.IP \fBend\fR 12
Upper bound on the position where a match may \fIstart\fR (optional; requires \fBstart\fR to
also be given)
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Called with 2 arguments: index of the first occurrence anywhere in \fBstring\fR, or
.B 0
if not found (see NOTES \(em this is
.B not
\-1)
.IP \(bu 2
Called with 3 arguments: index of the first occurrence at or after \fBstart\fR, or \-1 if not
found
.IP \(bu 2
Called with 4 arguments: index of the first occurrence at or after \fBstart\fR whose starting
position is less than \fBend\fR, or \-1 if not found. \fBend\fR bounds where the match may
.I begin,
not where it must finish; a match that starts before \fBend\fR but extends past it is still
returned.
.RE
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if \fBstring\fR or \fBsubstr\fR is not a string, if \fBstart\fR or \fBend\fR
(when given) is not an integer, or if more than 4 arguments are passed
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR len (string)
Get the length of a value's string representation, in characters.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBstring\fR 12
Value to measure. Non-string values are converted to their string representation first
(the same conversion \fBto_string()\fR performs), so \fBlen()\fR does not require a string
argument.
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Integer length of the (possibly converted) string
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR char_at (string, index)
Get the character at a specific index in a string.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBstring\fR 12
Input string
.IP \fBindex\fR 12
Zero-based character index
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Single-character string, or empty string if \fBindex\fR is negative or out of bounds
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if \fBstring\fR is not a string or \fBindex\fR is not an integer
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR substr (string, start[, length])
Extract a substring from a string.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBstring\fR 12
Input string
.IP \fBstart\fR 12
Starting index (zero-based)
.IP \fBlength\fR 12
Number of characters to extract (optional; defaults to the remainder of the string from
\fBstart\fR onward)
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Extracted substring, or empty string if \fBstart\fR is negative or out of bounds
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if \fBstring\fR is not a string, if \fBstart\fR or \fBlength\fR (when given) is
not an integer, or if more than 3 arguments are passed
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR concat (string1, string2, ...)
Concatenate two or more values together, converting each to its string representation first.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBstring...\fR 12
Two or more values to concatenate (variadic); need not already be strings
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Concatenated string
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR to_upper (string)
Convert a string to uppercase using the C locale.
.RS
.PP
.B Returns:
Uppercase version of the string
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if the argument is not a string
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR to_lower (string)
Convert a string to lowercase using the C locale.
.RS
.PP
.B Returns:
Lowercase version of the string
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if the argument is not a string
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR starts_with (string, prefix)
Check if a string starts with a given prefix.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBstring\fR 12
String to check
.IP \fBprefix\fR 12
Prefix to match
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Boolean true if string starts with prefix, false otherwise (including when \fBprefix\fR is
longer than \fBstring\fR)
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if either argument is not a string
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR ends_with (string, suffix)
Check if a string ends with a given suffix.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBstring\fR 12
String to check
.IP \fBsuffix\fR 12
Suffix to match
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Boolean true if string ends with suffix, false otherwise (including when \fBsuffix\fR is
longer than \fBstring\fR)
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if either argument is not a string
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR split (string, delimiter)
Split a string into an array of substrings on every occurrence of a delimiter.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBstring\fR 12
String to split
.IP \fBdelimiter\fR 12
Delimiter string to split on
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Array of strings. If \fBdelimiter\fR is an empty string, the array contains the original
string unchanged, as a single element. Consecutive delimiters produce empty-string elements
in the result.
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if either argument is not a string
.RE
.SH STRINGBUILDER FUNCTIONS
StringBuilder provides efficient string concatenation for building large strings
incrementally. A StringBuilder is referred to by an integer handle returned from
.BR sb_new() .
.TP
.BR sb_new ()
Create a new, empty StringBuilder instance.
.RS
.PP
.B Returns:
Integer handle representing the StringBuilder
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR sb_append (handle, value)
Append a value to a StringBuilder, converting it to its string representation first.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBhandle\fR 12
StringBuilder handle from \fBsb_new()\fR
.IP \fBvalue\fR 12
Value to append; need not already be a string
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
The handle (for method chaining)
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if \fBhandle\fR is not a valid, currently-allocated StringBuilder handle
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR sb_prealloc (handle, capacity)
Reserve internal storage capacity for a StringBuilder ahead of time, to reduce reallocations
during subsequent appends. This is a performance hint; it does not change the StringBuilder's
current contents.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBhandle\fR 12
StringBuilder handle from \fBsb_new()\fR
.IP \fBcapacity\fR 12
Number of characters to reserve space for. Values less than or equal to 0 are silently
ignored (not an error).
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
The handle (for method chaining)
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if \fBhandle\fR is not a valid, currently-allocated StringBuilder handle, or if
\fBcapacity\fR is not an integer
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR sb_to_string (handle)
Convert a StringBuilder to a string without freeing it.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBhandle\fR 12
StringBuilder handle
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
The complete string built so far
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if \fBhandle\fR is not a valid, currently-allocated StringBuilder handle
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR sb_clear (handle)
Clear the contents of a StringBuilder without freeing it.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBhandle\fR 12
StringBuilder handle
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
The handle (for method chaining)
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if \fBhandle\fR is not a valid, currently-allocated StringBuilder handle
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR sb_free (handle)
Free a StringBuilder and return its final contents. The handle becomes eligible for reuse by
a future \fBsb_new()\fR call and must not be used again.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBhandle\fR 12
StringBuilder handle
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
The final string value
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if \fBhandle\fR is not a valid, currently-allocated StringBuilder handle
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.B Basic String Operations
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/str";

var text = "Hello, World!";
puts(len(text));              // 13
puts(char_at(text, 0));        // "H"
puts(substr(text, 7, 5));      // "World"
puts(to_upper(text));          // "HELLO, WORLD!"
puts(to_lower(text));          // "hello, world!"
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B String Checking
.PP
.nf
.RS
var filename = "document.txt";

if (ends_with(filename, ".txt")) {
    puts("Text file detected");
}

if (starts_with(filename, "doc")) {
    puts("Starts with 'doc'");
}
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B String Concatenation
.PP
.nf
.RS
var first = "Hello";
var last = "World";
var result = concat(first, ", ", last, "!");
puts(result);  // "Hello, World!"
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B Finding Substrings \(em Mind the Not-Found Value
.PP
.nf
.RS
var text = "hello world";

// 2-argument form: not-found returns 0, NOT -1
var a = find(text, "xyz");        // 0  (looks like index 0, but it's "not found")
var b = find(text, "hello");      // 0  (a real match at index 0)

// 3- and 4-argument forms: not-found returns -1
var c = find(text, "xyz", 0);     // -1
var d = find(text, "world", 0);   // 6

// Prefer the 3-argument form (with an explicit start of 0) if you need to
// reliably distinguish "found at index 0" from "not found".
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B Splitting Strings
.PP
.nf
.RS
var csv = "one,two,,four";
var parts = split(csv, ",");
// parts == ["one", "two", "", "four"]
for (var i: i64 = 0; i < parts.length; i++) {
    puts(parts[i]);
}
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B StringBuilder Usage
.PP
.nf
.RS
var sb = sb_new();
sb_append(sb, "Line 1\\n");
sb_append(sb, "Line 2\\n");
sb_append(sb, "Line 3\\n");

var result = sb_to_string(sb);
puts(result);
sb_free(sb);
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B StringBuilder Chaining
.PP
.nf
.RS
var sb = sb_new();
sb_append(sb_append(sb_append(sb, "A"), "B"), "C");
puts(sb_to_string(sb));  // "ABC"
sb_free(sb);
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B Building Large Strings
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/str";
include "std/type";
include "std/file";

var sb = sb_new();
sb_prealloc(sb, 8192); // reserve space up front to avoid reallocations
for (var i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
    sb_append(sb, to_string(i));
    sb_append(sb, "\\n");
}
var output = sb_free(sb);
fwrite("numbers.txt", output);
.RE
.fi
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
String indices are zero-based
.IP \(bu 2
Out-of-bounds access in
.BR char_at() " and " substr()
returns empty strings rather than throwing errors
.IP \(bu 2
.B find()
called with exactly 2 arguments returns
.B 0
when the substring is not found, which is indistinguishable from a genuine match at index 0.
The 3- and 4-argument forms return \-1 on failure instead. This inconsistency is a quirk of
the current implementation, not an intentional design choice \(em prefer
\fBfind(string, substr, 0)\fR over the bare 2-argument form when the result of the search
needs to be checked.
.IP \(bu 2
In the 4-argument form of
.BR find() ,
.B end
only limits where a match may
.I start;
it does not truncate the search or guarantee the match finishes before
.BR end .
.IP \(bu 2
StringBuilder is more efficient than repeated string concatenation for building large strings
.IP \(bu 2
Always free StringBuilders with
.B sb_free()
when done; freed handles are recycled by later \fBsb_new()\fR calls, so re-using a handle
after it has been freed will silently operate on an unrelated StringBuilder rather than
raising an error
.IP \(bu 2
StringBuilder handles are integers and can be stored in variables
.IP \(bu 2
Case conversion functions use standard C locale rules
